# Buttons

A button changes something or submits an answer. Its text names the outcome.

These are civica.uk reference implementations for component authors. They do not replace native Home Assistant behaviour or demonstrate a connection to a live home.

## One button contract, several source references

This is the shared button owner for civica-authored examples. GOV.UK and NHS guidance inform one local behaviour contract; their source entries are not separate installed components. The [forms overview](controls.html#choose-the-control-by-its-meaning) helps choose between a command, selection and navigation.

Keep the four variants on this page: primary, secondary, explicitly destructive and unavailable. They share request/outcome semantics and visible focus. Source-specific presentation must not turn a navigation link into a command or treat an accepted command as a confirmed state. Native Home Assistant controls retain their own verified capability and permission behaviour.

## When to use it

Use one main action for a local task, with quieter secondary actions. A household dashboard can contain several independent device tasks: it does not need one global primary button for the whole home.

## When not to use it

Use a link for navigation. Do not make every room tile look like a submit button, or use a red button merely because the selected brand palette is red.

## Home Assistant adaptation

Keep native service permissions and pending states. A command is not a confirmed device state: after an action, describe that a request was sent until the integration reports the result. The reference actions below only update example text. Destructive confirmation uses explicit wording and the fixed danger role; it is not a palette variant.

## Accessibility and behaviour

Use a real button, give non-submit controls type="button", and retain a visible focus indicator. Keep repeat actions at least 44px high in this system. Explain an unavailable action beside the control instead of relying on a faded disabled appearance. Preserve focus while a request completes.

